WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.566 --> 00:00:03.560
(bright music)

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:06.120
- [Narrator] A lunar eclipse
happens when the earth blocks

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:08.680
some or all of the sun's direct light

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:09.903
from reaching the moon.

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:15.403
This cosmic event only takes
place during a full moon,

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:19.970
which happens once every
29 and a half days,

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:22.040
or the length of one full orbit

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:23.623
of the moon around the earth.

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:28.593
So why don't we have
an eclipse every month?

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:31.810
The moon's orbit is tilted a few degrees

00:00:31.810 --> 00:00:33.183
in relation to the earth,

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:37.533
so the earth, moon, and
sun don't always align.

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:40.550
When the earth does eclipse the sun,

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:43.460
it casts two types of shadows on the moon,

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:46.313
a larger shadow, known as the penumbra,

00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:51.733
and a smaller, darker
shadow, known as the umbra.

00:00:53.390 --> 00:00:56.830
There are three types of lunar eclipses.

00:00:56.830 --> 00:00:59.470
The first is a total lunar eclipse,

00:00:59.470 --> 00:01:03.270
when the sun, moon, and earth
are in perfect alignment

00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:06.363
and the moon falls within
the earth's umbral shadow.

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:10.420
Total lunar eclipses are the most striking

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:14.820
of the three types because they
turn the moon a sunset red.

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:17.260
While shorter, blue wavelengths of light

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:20.090
are scattered outward by
the earth's atmosphere,

00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:23.620
longer, red wavelengths
are refracted, or bent,

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:26.593
inward toward the moon,
making it appear red.

00:01:27.610 --> 00:01:29.340
The brightness of the moon's red glow

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:31.510
depends upon how much dust and clouds

00:01:31.510 --> 00:01:33.500
are in the earth's atmosphere.

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:35.350
Following volcanic activity,

00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:37.240
ash can block out enough light

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:41.770
to render the moon a darker
red, or even near black.

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:44.500
A partial lunar eclipse, the second type,

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:46.700
occurs when the earth, moon, and sun

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:49.610
don't perfectly align, so only part

00:01:49.610 --> 00:01:52.590
of the moon passes into earth's umbra.

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:54.560
Earth's shadow appears very dark

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:56.603
on the side of the moon facing earth.

00:01:58.610 --> 00:02:01.310
Last, a penumbral lunar eclipse,

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:02.960
occurs when the moon passes

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:04.793
through earth's penumbral shadow.

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:10.400
The event is so subtle that
most people don't even notice.

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:13.743
The moon will appear just
slightly darker than normal.

00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:18.830
Lunar eclipses occur up
to three times a year

00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:20.870
and can be observed from the entire

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:23.100
nighttime half of the earth.

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:26.650
Unlike during a solar eclipse,
it's safe to look at the moon

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:30.020
with the naked eye during a lunar eclipse.

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:33.530
It is only because of the
distances of the sun and moon

00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:35.820
from the earth that we are able to witness

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:37.563
total lunar eclipses.

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:42.770
As the moon inches away
from the earth each year,

00:02:42.770 --> 00:02:45.480
one day, billions of years from now,

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:48.000
the moon will be too far
away to fall completely

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:50.350
within earth's umbral shadow.

00:02:50.350 --> 00:02:53.120
Until then, we can
occasionally enjoy seeing

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:55.380
our own planet's shadow cast upon

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:57.873
the largest object in the night sky.

